  "id": "Compound::DB00394 - Gene::866",
  "verbalization": "Beclomethasone, a therapeutic glucocorticoid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of nasal septum perforation and Type IV hypersensitivity reactions through its interaction with Ciclesonide, a related glucocorticoid. Both compounds bind to Serpina6, a serine protease inhibitor, suggesting a shared mechanism of action involving the regulation of proteolytic processes. The evidence from the three paths highlights that Beclomethasone and its analogs, such as Ciclesonide and Flunisolide, all lead to the activation of Serpina6 through a common pathway involving glucocorticoid receptors. This suggests that Serpina6 may play a role in the inflammatory and immune responses mediated by these drugs. The integration of these findings implies that Beclomethasone and its glucocorticoid analogs may exert their effects through a shared molecular mechanism, with Serpina6 acting as a key mediator in the downstream signaling cascade. The observed scores support a direct link between Beclomethasone and Serpina6, mediated through glucocorticoid receptor activation and subsequent proteolytic regulation.",
